<TopicPage slug="subjects/agriculture/intermediate/animal-husbandry">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Animal husbandry is <strong>applied physiology, genetics and economics wearing hooves</strong>:
    conversion ratios decide viability, breeding programmes compound gains, and welfare has
    become a measured production variable rather than an afterthought. Livestock turns
    grass, residues and by-products humans cannot eat into milk, eggs and meat — the
    ecological case — while feed conversion and methane mark its costs. This lesson covers
    the three management pillars the syllabus names — <strong>housing, nutrition,
    health</strong> — with the systems, breeding and welfare questions that wrap them.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="conversion, breeding, health, the systems spectrum" widget="equationStep" config={animalCfg} />

  <h2>Nutrition: the conversion arithmetic</h2>
  <p>
    The master metric is the <strong>feed conversion ratio</strong> (FCR): kilograms of feed
    per kilogram of live-weight gain. Chickens convert best (~1.7), pigs intermediate
    (~3), cattle worst (~6–10 for beef) — the trophic lesson's ten-percent law compounded
    through another level, partly offset by ruminants' superpower: <strong>rumen
    fermentation</strong>. A cow's four-chambered forestomach hosts microbes that digest
    cellulose — converting grass, hay and crop residues, indigestible to humans and pigs,
    into meat and milk. Ruminant nutrition therefore starts with feeding the microbes:
    fibre keeps the rumen working, protein feeds the bacterial population, and energy
    balance governs everything else (an under-fed dairy cow loses condition and milk
    together). Non-ruminants (poultry, pigs) need precisely formulated diets — energy
    (cereals, fats), protein with the right <strong>amino acid balance</strong>
    (soybean meal supplies lysine; synthetic amino acids now replace much of the soy),
    minerals, vitamins — with feed as 60–70% of production cost, which is why precision
    feeding pays. Water is the first nutrient and the most forgotten: a dairy cow drinks
    60–100 litres a day, and intake limits milk before any other factor. The
    <strong>nutrition–product link</strong> runs both ways: feed quality shows in the
    product (milk yield and butterfat track diet; omega-3 enrichment of eggs is feed
    engineering), and deficits show in the animal within weeks.
  </p>

  <h2>Housing: environment as infrastructure</h2>
  <p>
    Housing exists to control what weather does to productivity and health. The physics:
    animals have <strong>thermal comfort zones</strong> (a lactating cow's is roughly
    5–20 °C — she is a walking furnace; piglets, conversely, need 30–34 °C at birth —
    which is why farrowing rooms run heat lamps over cold-snouted mothers), and outside
    the zone, feed intake shifts to thermoregulation — heat-stressed dairy cows drop milk
    yield measurably and eat less. Ventilation is therefore the core engineering:
    removing heat, moisture (a cow exhales litres of water vapour daily), ammonia and
    disease aerosols, without draughts. Design choices map onto systems: <strong>open
    pasture</strong> with shade and water (low capital, weather-exposed),
    <strong>deep-litter barns</strong> (bedded packs, composting in place),
    <strong>freestall/cubicle sheds</strong> for dairy (individual beds plus a feed
    alley — the standard of modern temperate dairying), <strong>cages, barns and
    aviaries</strong> for poultry (the welfare-driven migration from cages to enriched
    and cage-free systems is the industry's live case), and climate-controlled houses for
    broilers. Manure is housing's second product and its biggest engineering problem:
    slurry stores must be covered and leak-proof (methane and nitrate are the escapes;
    the water lesson's rules apply), and the application side — injection, low-emission
    spreaders, timing to crop need — is where the nutrients return value instead of
    becoming runoff. Stocking density, floor type (foot health — lameness tracks wet concrete)
    and access to outdoors are the variables welfare audits score, and the space
    allowances are now written into law in several jurisdictions. Good housing is
    measured in dry bedding, clean air you can breathe yourself, and animals that
    neither pant nor huddle — visible diagnostics before any meter.
  </p>

  <h2>Health and biosecurity: prevention as economics</h2>
  <p>
    Livestock health is managed as a <strong>herd</strong> problem, not a series of sick
    individuals — because the economics are exponential: a foot-and-mouth incursion or an
    avian influenza wave wipes farms; a subclinical mastitis or worm burden shaves
    margins invisibly. <strong>Biosecurity</strong> is the first line: quarantine for
    incoming stock, closed herds, boot dips and dedicated clothing, rodent and wild-bird
    control, all-in/all-out batching with disinfection between groups — the disease
    lesson's chain-of-transmission logic, applied to gates and lorries.
    <strong>Vaccination schedules</strong> (clostridial diseases, respiratory complexes,
    foot-and-mouth in endemic regions) convert the immune system into farm
    infrastructure. <strong>Parasite control</strong> shows the evolution lesson
    perfectly: anthelmintic-resistant worms are now common in sheep and cattle zones
    worldwide, so modern programmes count eggs, dose by weight, leave refugia
    (deliberately untreated animals so susceptible genes dilute resistance — the IPM
    lesson's refuge strategy in a paddock). The <strong>antimicrobial stewardship</strong>
    turn is agriculture's biggest health reform: roughly 70% of the world's antibiotics
    have gone to animals, growth-promotion doses are now banned in the EU (2006) and
    increasingly elsewhere, and treatment is moving to diagnosis-led use — because
    resistance genes cross between animal and human microbiomes. One Health is not a
    slogan here; it is a shared gene pool. And <strong>welfare doubles as
    diagnostics</strong>: lameness scoring, body-condition scoring, behaviour records —
    the Five Freedoms framework (freedom from hunger, discomfort, pain, fear, to express
    normal behaviour) has hardened into measurable indicators that correlate with
    productivity, which is why the welfare–profit "trade-off" is smaller than either side
    of the old debate claimed.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> "grain-fed means better-fed." Cattle cannot thrive on
    grain alone — rumen acidosis is the feedlot vet's daily case — and much of the
    world's ruminant production is precisely valuable because it runs on land and
    by-products unsuited to crops. The honest nutritional comparison is not "grass vs
    grain" but "what could this land grow otherwise?" — which is a systems question, not
    a feed label.</p>

  <h2>Breeding: the compounding programme</h2>
  <p>
    Selective breeding is husbandry's slow machine, and it compounds. Broiler chickens
    reached market weight (about 2.5 kg) in 12 weeks in the 1950s; the modern bird does it
    in under 6 weeks on a third less feed — cumulative genetic gain of a few percent per
    year, compounding into a transformed animal. Dairy cows average several times their
    1950s' milk yields, through a machine built of records and selections:
    performance-recording schemes (milk yield, fertility, longevity, now methane output),
    <strong>artificial insemination</strong> (one elite bull can sire hundreds of thousands
    of daughters — the genetic logistics that made national programmes possible), embryo
    transfer, and now <strong>genomic selection</strong>: genotyping calves at birth and
    predicting lifetime performance from marker data, halving the generation interval
    again. The breeding lesson's heritability arithmetic runs underneath — traits like
    growth rate respond fast; fertility and disease resistance respond slowly (lower
    heritability) — which is why balanced selection indices exist: pure production
    selection bred animals that grew brilliantly and fell apart reproductively, an
    expensive lesson the industry paid for in the 1990s. Modern indices weight
    longevity, health and foot traits alongside yield; and the welfare debates
    (broiler growth rates, dairy cow metabolic load) are now breeding criteria in
    several national programmes — genetics as the permanent, cumulative layer of
    welfare improvement.
  </p>

  <h2>A worked health case: mastitis</h2>
  <p>
    Mastitis — udder infection, the costliest dairy disease — shows the
    prevention-as-economics logic end to end. The path: bacteria (contagious ones like
    <em>Staph. aureus</em> spreading cow-to-cow at milking; environmental ones like
    <em>E. coli</em> picked up from dirty bedding) enter the teat canal; the udder
    inflames; milk somatic cell counts rise; yield drops, milk is withheld, and severe
    cases cost the quarter or the cow. The economics: a clinical case costs roughly
    150–300 € in treatment, withheld milk and lost future yield, and subclinical cases —
    invisible, but raising cell counts and cutting yield — cost more in aggregate than
    the clinical ones. The control stack is IPM-shaped: hygiene at milking (clean,
    dry teats; post-milking disinfection dips; the milking machine's pulsation checked),
    bedding management (dry bedding is the single strongest environmental lever),
    culling chronic carriers, selective dry-cow therapy (antibiotic only where a test
    says the cow needs it — the stewardship turn in one protocol), and genetics
    (somatic cell score is a heritable trait on every national index). The lesson
    generalises: nearly every major livestock disease — mastitis, lameness, respiratory
    complexes in calves — is a management-system disease first and an infection second;
    the vet treats the case, the husbandry treats the rate.
  </p>

  <h2>Systems: the spectrum from pasture to intensive</h2>
  <p>
    No single system is "the answer"; each matches a factor endowment.
    <strong>Extensive/pasture systems</strong> — ranching, upland sheep, pastoralism —
    run on land that could grow little else: low inputs, low outputs per hectare of
    labour, high per-product footprint where land is scarce, but irreplaceable where
    grass is the only crop. <strong>Mixed crop–livestock systems</strong> — historically
    the default and still most of the world's farming — are the nutrient-cycling
    engine: crop residues feed animals; manure feeds fields; the rotation feeds both
    (the soil lesson's fertility account, deposited on hooves). <strong>Intensive
    systems</strong> — broiler houses, pig units, high-input dairies, feedlots —
    maximise efficiency per animal and per worker, at the cost of capital,
    concentrated waste streams (a 10,000-cow dairy produces the sewage of a town),
    disease-pressure management and welfare scrutiny. <strong>Aquaculture</strong>
    joined the spectrum quietly and hugely: over half of eaten seafood is now farmed,
    the fastest-growing protein sector, with its own FCR economics (carp near 1.5,
    salmon ~1.2 after two decades of feed improvement) and disease lessons
    (sea lice, vaccination now central). The environmental accounting is double-entry:
    <strong>per kilogram of product</strong>, intensification usually wins (less land,
    less feed per kg); <strong>per hectare and per litre of water</strong>, extensive
    and mixed systems often win. Climate adds the methane column — ruminant enteric
    fermentation is about a quarter of livestock's ~12–14.5% share of global emissions,
    and feed additives (3-NOP, seaweed-derived bromoform) now cut enteric methane
    20–30% in trials — the sector's fastest lever, scaling now.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    The measurement layer is where husbandry is converging with data science.
    <strong>Sensor-driven management</strong>: rumen boluses log temperature and pH,
    accelerometers detect lameness and oestrus (heat detection by algorithm outperforms
    human observation; fertility is the margin), milk meters read every cow twice a day,
    and walk-over weighing tracks growth without handling — precision livestock farming
    is the field lesson's sibling. Disease intelligence is going the same way: syndromic
    surveillance (milk yields dropping across a region flagged the 2010s Schmallenberg
    virus before formal reports), rapid molecular diagnostics at the gate, and genomic
    epidemiology tracing foot-and-mouth lineages in hours. The welfare frontier is
    moving from freedoms to <em>positive states</em> — play, grooming, social comfort —
    with validated behavioural measures, and the market is ahead of the law: cage-free
    pledges by major retailers have restructured the US egg industry faster than any
    regulation. Labour completes the systems arithmetic: intensification is largely
    substitution of capital and data for skilled hands, and the farms that do best on
    welfare audits are, in study after study, the ones with the most trained stockpeople
    — gentle handling measurably raises growth and fertility, one of the few results
    replicated across species. The deepest open question remains the dual one this
    lesson keeps meeting: how to produce more animal protein for a growing, wealthier
    population
    (global demand is projected to rise for decades) while cutting land, water and
    climate costs — the answer being assembled from all of this lesson's parts:
    conversion efficiency, better genetics, prevention over cure, methane levers, and
    systems matched honestly to the land they stand on.
  </p>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> FAO livestock assessments; the Five Freedoms (Farm Animal Welfare Council); trophic and genetics lessons; Cambridge IGCSE Agriculture 0600, unit 7.</p>
</TopicPage>
